        <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="m19 9-5 5-4-4-3 3"/></svg>
      </span>
      <div class="min-w-0">
        <h3 class="t-h3" style="margin:0">Effective levels — general guide</h3>
        <p class="t-small" style="margin:2px 0 0">The concentrations these actives are <em>typically</em> effective at in research — not a measurement of this product.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px">
        <div style="border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:10px;padding:12px 14px">
          <div class="flex items-center" style="gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:4px">
            <span style="font-weight:700;color:var(--color-text)">Niacinamide</span>
            <span class="tnum" style="font-weight:700;font-size:13px;color:var(--color-primary-600);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--color-primary-600) 12%,#fff);border-radius:999px;padding:2px 10px">2–5% (up to 10%)</span>
          </div>
          <p class="t-small" style="margin:0 0 4px">Most research uses 2–5%; some formulas go to 10%. Very high levels can cause flushing in sensitive skin.</p>
          <p class="t-caption" style="margin:0;color:var(--color-text-subtle)">Niacinamide</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="t-caption" style="margin:14px 0 0;color:var(--color-text-muted)">
      <strong>INCI lists don't disclose amounts</strong>, and we don't claim to know this product's levels —
      these are the ranges these ingredients are usually effective at, so you can tell a real formula from
      "fairy-dusting" a marketed active.
      <a data-turbolinks="false" href="/methodology#concentration">How we estimate this</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

            

